WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 256: The Wright Brothers: Orville and Wilbur, the Kitty Hawk Dunes, and the Conquest of the Air

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.200
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series chronicles the fulfillment of humanity's oldest, most impossible dream.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.760
[Rebecca] Two self-taught brothers from Dayton, Ohio, who unlocked the secrets of the air and gave mankind wings.

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary story of Wilbur and Orville Wright.

6
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:45.760
[George] In 1878, Bishop Milton Wright of the Church of the United Brethren in Christ returned home from a church conference in Dayton, Ohio, carrying a small gift for his young sons Wilbur and Orville, a toy helicopter made of cork, bamboo, and thin paper.

7
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.920
[George] Powered by a twisted rubber band designed by French aviation pioneer Alpense Peinaude.

8
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.040
[George] The boys threw the toy into the air, completely enchanted as it fluttered to the ceiling.

9
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.920
[George] Orville later recalled that the toy sparked their lifelong fascination with flight.

10
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.880
[George] Wilbur was introspective, scholarly, and articulate, possessing a formidable intellect and iron will.

11
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.840
[George] Orville was cheerful, mechanically ingenious, and a brilliant craftsman.

12
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:24.560
[George] Neither brother ever married, and they lived together in complete, selfless fraternal harmony, famously thinking, arguing, and working together as a single unified mind.

13
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:37.280
[George] In 1892, amid the nationwide cycling craze, the brothers opened the Wright Cycle Company on West 3rd Street in Dayton, designing and manufacturing their own high-quality Van Cleve and St.

14
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.800
[George] Clair bicycles.

15
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:53.360
[George] Working with precision bearings, chains, and tubular frames, the brothers developed a masterly practical knowledge of mechanical balance, speed, and weight distribution that proved to be the decisive foundation of their aeronautical breakthroughs.

16
00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:06.600
[George] Bishop Wright's insistence on independent intellectual inquiry, rigorous moral integrity, and familial devotion created an extraordinary household where the brothers felt free to challenge established scientific dogma.

17
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:18.600
[George] The Van Cleve bicycle was renowned throughout Dayton for its precision machining, hardened steel bearings, and oil-retaining hubs, showcasing the brothers' exceptional mechanical craftsmanship.

18
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:28.520
[George] In 1896, when German gliding pioneer Otto Lilienthal was killed in a glider crash, the Wright brothers resolved to tackle the problem of flight.

19
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:44.200
[George] While other experimenters like Samuel Langley of the Smithsonian Institution focused on constructing massive engines to muscle inherently stable machines through the sky, the Wrights realized that flight was primarily a problem of three-dimensional dynamic control.

20
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:52.680
[George] An aviator had to be able to bank, turn, and stabilize a machine in shifting winds exactly like a cyclist balances a bicycle.

21
00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] In the summer of 1899, while twisting an empty cardboard bicycle inner tube box in his hands in the shop, Wilbur conceived wing warping, twisting the trailing edges of biplane wings in opposite directions to vary aerodynamic lift on either side, allowing the pilot to bank and recover lateral balance.

22
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:23.720
[George] Seeking a remote testing ground with strong, steady ocean breezes, soft sand dunes for cushion landings, and isolation from prying newspaper reporters, the brothers wrote to the U.S.

23
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:31.240
[George] Weather Bureau, which recommended the windswept barrier islands of the outer banks at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.

24
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Between 1900 and 1901, the brothers lived in canvas tents amid sandstorms and ferocious mosquito swarms, testing biplane gliders with 50-foot wingspans, lying prone on the lower wing to minimize drag.

25
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:56.160
[George] Wilbur's cardboard inner tube box breakthrough revealed that birds do not flap their wings to maintain balance in flight, but twist their wingtips to interact dynamically with the air.

26
00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:07.920
[George] The remote fishing hamlet of Kitty Hawk, with its towering sand dunes and isolation from eastern newspaper reporters, provided the ideal crucible for their dangerous aerodynamic experiments.

27
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.600
[George] The summer glider trials of 1901 at Kill Devil Hills ended in bitter disappointment.

28
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:26.400
[George] Their glider produced barely a third of the lift predicted by the scientific tables published by John Smeaton and Otto Lilienthal, and when banked into a turn, the glider frequently slid sideways into the sand.

29
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.120
[George] Returning to Dayton, Wilbur remarked to Orville in sorrow that man would not fly for another thousand years.

30
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:48.960
[George] But the brothers refused to quit, recognizing that the published scientific tables of aerodynamic lift and drag were fundamentally flawed, they constructed their own six-foot wooden wind tunnel powered by a two-cylinder gas engine in the back of their bicycle shop.

31
00:04:48.960 --> 00:05:04.720
[George] Fabricating tiny precision balances from old hacksaw blades and bicycle spokes, the brothers systematically tested over 200 different airfoil shapes, measuring camber, aspect ratios and surface curvature with unprecedented mathematical rigor.

32
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:17.680
[George] Their wind tunnel experiments produced the first accurate aerodynamic data in world history, establishing the precise camber curves of modern aircraft wings and proving that world science had been wrong for a century.

33
00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] The miniature wind tunnel built from a bicycle spoke balance and a wooden starch box was an engineering masterpiece, delivering mathematical precision that surpassed the world's leading academic institutions.

34
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:40.760
[George] Their wind tunnel measurements proved that Otto Lilienthal's published lift tables were in error by nearly 30%, explaining why earlier gliders had plummeted to the sand.

35
00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:53.560
[George] Armed with their revolutionary wind tunnel data, the Wrights returned to Kitty Hawk in September 1902 with a majestic new glider featuring a 32-foot wingspan and an entirely redesigned airfoil.

36
00:05:53.560 --> 00:06:01.000
[George] But when they made gliding turns, the machine experienced a terrifying aerodynamic anomaly known as adverse yaw.

37
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:08.680
[George] The wing with higher lift produced greater drag, pulling the nose of the glider away from the turn and causing it to spin into the dune.

38
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:13.000
[George] One night in October, Orville woke up with a brilliant solution.

39
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:24.920
[George] Transformed the fixed twin rear rudders into a single movable vertical rudder, mechanically interconnected with the wing warping cables so that banking the wings automatically turned the tail to counteract yaw.

40
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.440
[George] It was the crowning conceptual breakthrough of aeronautics.

41
00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:37.400
[George] Three-axis control, pitch controlled by the front horizontal elevator, roll controlled by wing warping, and yaw controlled by the rear rudder.

42
00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:50.920
[George] Flying their 1902 glider in over a thousand soaring glides covering distances of 600 feet, the Wright brothers mastered the skies, flying with complete stability and confidence in 25 mile an hour gales.

43
00:06:50.920 --> 00:07:03.400
[George] The movable vertical rudder interconnected with the wing warping cables solved adverse yaw, establishing the three axis flight control system that remains the standard for every modern aircraft flying today.

44
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:14.640
[George] Flying their 1902 glider over the killed devil dunes, the brothers logged more time and controlled gliding flight than all other aviation experimenters in human history combined.

45
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.840
[George] All that remained was to add an engine and propellers.

46
00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:37.280
[George] Finding no automobile manufacturer capable of building an engine light and powerful enough, the brothers collaborated with their gifted shop mechanic Charlie Taylor, designing and casting a 12-horsepower four-cylinder gasoline engine made with an aluminum crankcase, weighing barely 180 pounds.

47
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:48.960
[George] Designing their twin eight-foot wooden propellers as rotating aerodynamic wings using their wind tunnel tables, the brothers assembled the Wright Flyer at Kill Devil Hills in late 1903.

48
00:07:48.960 --> 00:08:04.400
[George] On the freezing, gusty morning of Thursday, December 17, 1903, with an icy 27 mile an hour wind whipping across the sand, five local lifesavers from the Killed Devil Hills life-saving station gathered to witness history.

49
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.480
[George] Orville won the coin toss, taking his prone position on the lower wing beside the chugging engine.

50
00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.720
[George] At 10.35 a.m., the restraining wire was released.

51
00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:25.520
[George] The flyer rolled 60 feet down the wooden launch rail, lifted into the air, and flew for 12 seconds, covering 120 feet before touching down safely in the sand.

52
00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.360
[George] Lifesaver John T.

53
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.960
[George] Daniels captured the moment on a glass plate camera.

54
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.760
[George] The immortal photograph of the flyer in mid-air with Wilbur running alongside.

55
00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:47.840
[George] Making three more flights that day, Wilbur piloted the fourth flight over 852 feet in 59 seconds, achieving the first controlled, sustained-powered flight in human history.

56
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:58.720
[George] Charlie Taylor's hand-built aluminum crankcase engine delivered 12 reliable horsepower, providing the precise power-to-weight ratio needed to lift the wooden flyer into the air.

57
00:08:58.720 --> 00:08:59.760
[George] John T.

58
00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:09.400
[George] Daniel's glass plate photograph captured the exact moment the flyer left the ground, freezing in time the historic dawn of the modern aerial century.

59
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:18.360
[George] Returning to Ohio, the Wrights continued perfecting their aircraft in an 84-acre cow pasture 8 miles east of Dayton known as Huffman Prairie.

60
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:27.000
[George] Flying the improved Flyer II and Flyer III, the brothers practiced banking turns, figurates, and landing without damage.

61
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:37.960
[George] By October 1905, Wilbur flew the Flyer III continuously for 38 minutes, covering 24 miles around the pasture until his fuel tank was exhausted.

62
00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:40.680
[George] Yet the American press and the U.S.

63
00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:48.680
[George] war department refused to believe them, dismissing reports of powered flight as impossible exaggerations or outright fraud.

64
00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:59.400
[George] For two years, the brothers kept their airplane packed in wooden crates, refusing to fly in public until they had secured international patents and government purchase contracts.

65
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:12.600
[George] European aviation enthusiasts openly mocked the Americans, claiming that the Wright brothers were bluffers who had never left the ground and that French aviators like Alberto Santos Dumont were the true masters of the air.

66
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:23.880
[George] Huffman Prairie's muddy cow pasture was the secret cradle of practical aviation, where the brothers mastered complete 360-degree banking turns and passenger carrying capability.

67
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:35.480
[George] The skepticism of the American press was exemplified by the Dayton Daily News, whose editor famously refused to send a reporter to Huffman Prairie, believing powered flight was impossible.

68
00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] In August 1908, 31-year-old Wilbur Wright arrived at the Hunedier's racecourse in Le Mans, France, to silence the world's skeptics.

69
00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:58.560
[George] As thousands of spectators and cynical French aviators watched, Wilbur fired up the flyer, took off gracefully, and carved effortless, banking figurates in the French sky, landing softly after two minutes.

70
00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:02.720
[George] The European aviation world was stunned into humble adoration.

71
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:10.240
[George] French pioneers wept openly, newspaper headlines proclaimed We Are Beaten, and the Wrights were hailed as gods.

72
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:18.880
[George] Simultaneously, Orville demonstrated the flyer before thousands of military officers at Fort Meyer, Virginia, winning the first U.S.

73
00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.880
[George] Army aviation contract.

74
00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:36.880
[George] Tragically, Wilbur contracted typhoid fever and died in May 1912 at age 45, while Orville lived until 1948, witnessing aviation evolve from their 12-second hop into supersonic jet bombers and global commercial transport.

75
00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:52.160
[George] The Wright brothers proved that genius is not the monopoly of universities or governments, but the product of quiet humility, unshakable brotherly devotion, and relentless scientific truth, the two bicycle mechanics who gave humanity the sky.

76
00:11:52.160 --> 00:12:05.200
[George] Wilbur's breathtaking aerial demonstrations at Le Mans, France, completely silenced international doubters, reducing seasoned French aviators to tears of admiration at his effortless mastery of the sky.

77
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:17.160
[George] The Wright brothers' legacy proves that patient empirical testing, selfless fraternal cooperation, and unshakable courage can conquer the most daunting frontiers of the physical universe.

78
00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.760
[George] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

79
00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:28.080
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 257.

80
00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:34.280
[Rebecca] Henry Ford, the Quadricycle, The Model T, The $5 Day, and the Moving Assembly Line.

81
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:36.600
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

